id: 1151
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1151, "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "data": {"id": "cac9bae0-e04d-4dd8-b3aa-540383dc1a35", "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "kind": "user", "position": 1151, "revision": 1151, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "# Round 003 — factual feedback (operator, evidence-based only)\n\nYour Round 002 response did address most items: you read `SKILL.md` and the reference modules (raw JSONL lines 146–164), confirmed the rank-1/IC mis-join yourself (lines 166–177), launched two native general-purpose sub-agents (lines 178, 184), fixed the count errors, and the validator now passes. The delegation contract structure in both prompts matched `references/delegation-contract.md`. The items below are new facts found by auditing the saved verification code and results. No scientific answers are provided.\n\n## Observed facts and evidence locations\n\n1. **The independent FFT verification contains a frequency-unit conversion error, and its headline conclusion inherits it.** In `working/subagent_fft_loo/fft_loo_analysis.py`, line 147 computes `np.fft.rfftfreq(n, d=dt)` where `dt` is in **minutes** (`dt_minutes: 1.0` is stored in your own `fft_results.json` per-pass records), so `fft_freqs` is in **cycles per minute**. Line 161 then does `top_freqs_muhz = top_freqs_hz * 1e6` with the comment \"convert Hz to muHz\" — but the input is cycles/minute, not Hz. The correct factor from cycles/minute to μHz is 1e6/60; the script's values are therefore off by a factor of 60. Internal contradiction you can check without any external knowledge: your `fft_results.json` stores common signals at \"37500, 50000, 62500 μHz\" and per-pass peaks up to \"487500 μHz\", while the same file records `dt_minutes: 1.0` — with 1-minute sampling the Nyquist frequency is 1/(2·60 s) ≈ 8333 μHz, so no frequency above ~8333 μHz can appear from this data by any correct conversion. Result.md Finding 1 now repeats these numbers (\"Top FFT peak frequencies span 12,500–487,500 μHz\") and the conclusion \"no consistent signal near 1010 μHz\", which was computed on the mislabeled axis. You need to recheck the conversion, recompute, save corrected artifacts, and re-reconcile Finding 1 with whatever the corrected spectrum shows.\n\n2. **The period statement in Finding 1 is off by a factor of 3600.** `fft_loo_analysis.py` line 346: `target_period_min = 60.0 / (target_freq_muhz * 1e-6)` yields 59,405.9 for 1010 μHz, and Result.md states \"The target period of 1010 μHz (~59,400 minutes)\". From frequency in Hz, the period is 1/f seconds: 1/0.001010 s ≈ 990 s ≈ 16.5 minutes, which fits well inside an ~80-minute pass (about 4.8 cycles). The line-346 formula mixes seconds and minutes. The downstream claim \"no single pass has sufficient temporal resolution to resolve this frequency\" (also in the sub-agent receipt and Result.md) was derived from the wrong period; re-derive it and check it against the actual window length and bin resolution.\n\n3. **Main-side integration adopted the sub-agents' conclusions without reading their source code.** Your main raw JSONL shows Read calls on the three JSON artifacts (`fft_results.json`, `loo_results.json`, `verification_results.json` — lines 262–265) but no Read call on `fft_loo_analysis.py` or `independent_verification.py` before you revised Result.md around them (lines 285–308). `references/delegation-contract.md` §最终结果交接 requires the main side to read the final source code and results before treating completion as closed (\"主 Agent 将执行者的完成状态与最终落盘文件对应起来，再阅读关键源码与结果\"). The unit error above is exactly the failure mode this guards against: a summary that looks consistent while the underlying implementation is wrong.\n\n4. **What currently stands vs. what needs rework in the saved artifacts.** The LOO results (`loo_results.json`) are time-domain statistics in mm/s and are not affected by the FFT unit issue; the pass_01 outlier statement in Finding 2 traces to saved values. The cross-pool overlap finding (29/250 rows shared between the two candidate files) is in `verification_results.json`. But `result_artifacts.json` was updated (raw JSONL line 316–322) to embed the FFT section containing the mislabeled frequencies, so the machine-readable artifact now carries the same unit error as the report.\n\n## Required actions (yours to execute)\n\n- Fix the unit conversion in the FFT verification (and the period formula at line 346), rerun the corrected computation, save corrected artifacts, and update Result.md Finding 1, the executive summary, `working/analysis-state.md`, and `result_artifacts.json` so all four agree with the corrected numbers.\n- Read both sub-agent scripts yourself before the next revision, record in analysis-state.md which executor produced which version and what you verified.\n- Re-run `python3 validate_output.py output` after the corrections and keep the output.\n"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:25:53.619316+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:25:53.619322+00:00"}}

