id: 1195
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1195, "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "data": {"id": "06ebfe74-8aea-4f74-88e0-bef016f0f63e", "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "kind": "text", "position": 1195, "revision": 1195, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "Excellent! The corrected FFT reveals important new findings:\n\n1. **Nyquist frequency is ~8,333 μHz** (not 487,500 as the buggy script claimed"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:16.808379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:16.808385+00:00"}}

id: 1196
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1196, "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "data": {"id": "06ebfe74-8aea-4f74-88e0-bef016f0f63e", "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "kind": "text", "position": 1195, "revision": 1196, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "Excellent! The corrected FFT reveals important new findings:\n\n1. **Nyquist frequency is ~8,333 μHz** (not 487,500 as the buggy script claimed) — all frequencies are within valid range\n2. **1040 μHz appears in 17 of 22 passes** as a top-5 peak — this is very close to the rank-1 frequency of 1010 μHz"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:16.808379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:17.317592+00:00"}}

id: 1197
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1197, "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "data": {"id": "06ebfe74-8aea-4f74-88e0-bef016f0f63e", "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "kind": "text", "position": 1195, "revision": 1197, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "Excellent! The corrected FFT reveals important new findings:\n\n1. **Nyquist frequency is ~8,333 μHz** (not 487,500 as the buggy script claimed) — all frequencies are within valid range\n2. **1040 μHz appears in 17 of 22 passes** as a top-5 peak — this is very close to the rank-1 frequency of 1010 μHz!\n3. **620 μHz and 830 μHz** also appear frequently (23 and 19 appearances) — these are harmonics/subharmonics\n4. **All 22 passes can resolve 1010 μHz** (Nyquist = 8,333 μHz >> 1,0"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:16.808379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:17.829537+00:00"}}

id: 1200
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1200, "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "data": {"id": "06ebfe74-8aea-4f74-88e0-bef016f0f63e", "run_id": "run_019c2e0ca17d4274", "kind": "text", "position": 1195, "revision": 1200, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "Excellent! The corrected FFT reveals important new findings:\n\n1. **Nyquist frequency is ~8,333 μHz** (not 487,500 as the buggy script claimed) — all frequencies are within valid range\n2. **1040 μHz appears in 17 of 22 passes** as a top-5 peak — this is very close to the rank-1 frequency of 1010 μHz!\n3. **620 μHz and 830 μHz** also appear frequently (23 and 19 appearances) — these are harmonics/subharmonics\n4. **All 22 passes can resolve 1010 μHz** (Nyquist = 8,333 μHz >> 1,010 μHz; window spans ~79 min >> 16.5 min period)\n5. **Target period is 16.5 minutes**, not 59,400 minutes — well within each pass's ~79-minute span\n\nThis significantly changes Finding 1. Let me now read the corrected results file and update everything:\n\n"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:16.808379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:18.496417+00:00"}}

